# Kinematics Shared Library

This allows Python to call the C++ kinematics calculations from the FluidNC firmware directly, without rewriting them. If the kinematics change, recompile the library and everything stays in sync.

## Prerequisites

- `g++` with C++17 support
- The FluidNC firmware source code

## Repository Setup

The firmware repo should be available as a git submodule or sibling directory. All commands below assume you are running them from the **firmware repo root** (where `src/` and `platformio.ini` live).

## Compilation

### Linux (x86_64)

```bash
g++ -shared -fPIC -I. -Istubs -o kinematics.so \
  kinematics_wrapper.cpp \
  stubs/globals.cpp \
  $(find src/Kinematics -name "*.cpp")
```

### macOS (Apple Silicon / arm64)

```bash
g++ -shared -fPIC -arch arm64 -I. -Istubs -o kinematics.dylib \
  kinematics_wrapper.cpp \
  stubs/globals.cpp \
  $(find src/Kinematics -name "*.cpp")
```

### macOS (Intel / x86_64)

```bash
g++ -shared -fPIC -arch x86_64 -I. -Istubs -o kinematics.dylib \
  kinematics_wrapper.cpp \
  stubs/globals.cpp \
  $(find src/Kinematics -name "*.cpp")
```

After compiling, copy the output file (`kinematics.so` or `kinematics.dylib`) to wherever your Python code can find it.

## What the stubs/ folder is

The firmware is written for an ESP32 microcontroller and depends on embedded headers (FreeRTOS, Arduino, ESP32 SDK) that don't exist on a desktop PC. The `stubs/` folder contains minimal fake versions of those headers — just enough to satisfy the compiler. They have no effect on the firmware itself and are never compiled into it.

## Recompiling after kinematics changes

Just rerun the compile command and copy the new `.so`/`.dylib` to your Python project. No other changes needed.